# Go tooling assumes LF. Without this, a checkout on Windows with core.autocrlf
# converts sources to CRLF and `gofmt -l .` reports every file, breaking the
# quality gate for reasons that have nothing to do with the code.
*.go text eol=lf
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Fixture sources, for a sharper reason than the above: testdata/manifest.json
# records a SHA-256 for every generated fixture, and a .tex that arrives CRLF on one
# checkout and LF on another rebuilds to a different PDF. Pinning the source pins the
# artifact the hash describes.
*.tex text eol=lf

# Binary fixtures must never be line-ending converted.
*.pdf binary
*.gguf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary

# Embedded font assets, for the same reason and with a sharper consequence: a .ttf.gz that gets
# line-ending converted on checkout does not inflate, so every substituted page in that clone is
# refused with a reason naming a face the binary is in fact carrying. internal/liberation's
# load-and-parse test catches it, which is why that test reads all eight faces rather than trusting
# the embed.
*.ttf binary
*.ttf.gz binary
*.gz binary
